  "text": "I'll start by examining the current state of the files I can edit.Now let me verify against a temporary geometry implementation (without touching the frozen `geometry.py` stub).I'll keep the harness inside the workspace root instead (outside the claimed `src/` files).`src/report.py` is implemented. Summary of this round:\n\n**Decision & rationale:** `src/geometry.py` was claimed by w1 (and is the lower layer), so I took the upper layer, `src/report.py`. I implemented `describe()` as a pure formatter that delegates all shape math to the already-imported `geometry.area`/`geometry.perimeter`. This deliberately avoids duplicating any shape logic, so `report` and `geometry` can't drift out of sync, and `ValueError` for unknown `kind`s propagates naturally from geometry without `report` needing its own kind checks.\n\n**Precise format:** I chose a single f-string producing `f\"{shape['kind']}: area={area(shape):.2f} perimeter={perimeter(shape):.2f}\"` on one line, so `describe(rect(3,4))` → `\"rect: area=12.00 perimeter=14.00\"` and `describe(circle(1))` → `\"circle: area=3.14 perimeter=6.28\"`.\n\n**Verification:** I wrote a throwaway harness at [`_verify_report_check.py`](file:///tmp/lightgravity-worktrees/agensh-sdk-20261015/probes/agensh-orchestration-sdk/runs/formal/t2_interface__B__r0/staging/r1/w2/_verify_report_check.py) that monkeypatches `report.area`/`report.perimeter` with a reference implementation, so `describe()` could be exercised independently of w1's progress. It asserts exact string equality for rect and circle, and asserts a `ValueError` propagates for an unknown `kind`. (Note: file-writing tools are restricted to the workspace, so the harness lives at the workspace root rather than `/tmp`; it is not part of the deliverable and can be deleted. I could not execute it — I have no shell tool in this environment — so it is written for a runner to execute.)\n\nI did not modify `src/geometry.py` or any other file.Finished",
